WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
How can a leaf pull carbon from air, build sugar, and still keep its carbon-catching

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:10.760
machine running? Follow the carbon count, and the energy bill will reveal itself.

3
00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:20.913
The Calvin cycle runs in chloroplast stroma, using ATP and NADPH from light

4
00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:21.913
reactions.

5
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:28.037
Rubisco joins one CO2 to RuBP, a five-carbon acceptor.

6
00:00:29.137 --> 00:00:33.857
The brief six-carbon intermediate immediately splits into two three-carbon molecules

7
00:00:33.857 --> 00:00:37.257
of 3-phosphoglycerate, or 3-PGA.

8
00:00:38.857 --> 00:00:42.257
Fixation itself spends no ATP or NADPH.

9
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.960
Both 3-PGA molecules must become the triose sugar G3P.

10
00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:55.760
Each conversion spends one ATP to prime the acid and one NADPH to reduce it.

11
00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.840
So far, one CO2 has cost 2 ATP and 2 NADPH.

12
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:06.793
But can both new trioses leave while the conveyor keeps turning?

13
00:01:08.233 --> 00:01:11.473
Count the carbons needed to rebuild each five-carbon acceptor.

14
00:01:16.967 --> 00:01:18.527
Scale up to 3 turns.

15
00:01:19.887 --> 00:01:26.427
3 RuBP plus 3 CO2 make 6 trioses, holding 18 carbons altogether.

16
00:01:27.887 --> 00:01:31.767
Only 3 carbons came from air, so only 1 triose is net gain.

17
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:38.850
The other 5 trioses hold 15 carbons, exactly enough to rebuild 3 five-carbon

18
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.230
acceptors. The counters are reshuffled, not discarded.

19
00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:49.990
Regeneration then spends 3 more ATP to remake RuBP and close all 3 loops.

20
00:01:51.133 --> 00:01:53.513
Now divide that 3-turn bill by 3.

21
00:01:54.693 --> 00:02:00.113
At steady state, the ordinary C3 cycle costs 3 ATP per CO2.

22
00:02:01.513 --> 00:02:03.453
It also costs 2 NADPH.

23
00:02:04.733 --> 00:02:09.253
One extra ATP beyond reduction pays to rebuild the acceptor.

24
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:12.333
Count the carbons, and the bill follows.

25
00:02:13.467 --> 00:02:19.707
3 turns therefore spend 9 ATP and 6 NADPH for 1 net G3P.

26
00:02:20.787 --> 00:02:26.007
6 turns spend 18 ATP and 12 NADPH for 2 net trioses.

27
00:02:27.327 --> 00:02:32.407
Those trioses can supply 1 hexose phosphate at the same Calvin-cycle cost.

28
00:02:33.647 --> 00:02:36.747
The plant does not directly release free glucose here.

29
00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.160
This is the simplest steady-state picture.

30
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:46.480
Startup, photorespiration, and carbon-concentrating pathways change the accounting;

31
00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:49.160
the page below develops those cases.

32
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:54.710
A leaf builds carbon product only while rebuilding its carbon catcher.

33
00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:02.170
That is why 1 triose in 6 is net, and why ATP demand exceeds NADPH demand.
